{"dish/doria-food":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/doria-food","n":"Doria (food)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Yoshoku","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Doria is a type of rice gratin popular in Japan. Cooked white rice is topped with sautéed meat, such as chicken or shrimp, and vegetables, then topped with a béchamel sauce and cheese, and baked as a casserole. Doria is an example of yōshoku, Western food tailored to Japanese tastes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-doria-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doria_(food)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Yoshoku","cuisine/yoshoku"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Béchamel","ingredient/bechamel"],["Béchamel sauce","ingredient/bechamel-sauce"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Pilaf","ingredient/pilaf"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]},{"h":"More from Yoshoku","links":[["Chicken katsu","dish/chicken-katsu"],["Cream stew","dish/cream-stew"],["Hayashi rice","dish/hayashi-rice"],["Menchi-katsu","dish/menchi-katsu"],["Naporitan","dish/naporitan"],["Tonkatsu","dish/tonkatsu"],["Toruko rice","dish/toruko-rice"],["Volga rice","dish/volga-rice"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/e/e7/Meat_doria.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"投稿者本人による撮影","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meat_doria.jpg"}},"dish/garak-guksu":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/garak-guksu","n":"Garak-guksu","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Korean cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["In Korean cuisine, garak-guksu are thick wheat noodles and noodle dishes made with thick noodles."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-garak-guksu"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garak-guksu"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Korean cuisine","cuisine/korean-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Maangchi","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"maangchi.com","note":"Maangchi's Korean recipe site and YouTube channel, one of the best known English sources for Korean cooking.","url":"https://www.maangchi.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Korean Bapsang","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"koreanbapsang.com","note":"Home-style Korean recipes from Hyosun, a Korean-American mom.","url":"https://www.koreanbapsang.com/category/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Korean cuisine","links":[["Bap (rice dish)","dish/bap-rice-dish"],["Bibim-guksu","dish/bibim-guksu"],["Bibimbap","dish/bibimbap"],["Bosintang","dish/bosintang"],["Cheonggukjang","dish/cheonggukjang"],["Doenjang","dish/doenjang"],["Gochujang","dish/gochujang"],["Gopchang","dish/gopchang"],["Hwajeon","dish/hwajeon"],["Jijimi","dish/jijimi"],["Jokbal","dish/jokbal"],["Korean restaurant","dish/korean-restaurant"],["Maekjeok","dish/maekjeok"],["Maeun-tang","dish/maeun-tang"],["Makchang","dish/makchang"],["Mandu-guk","dish/mandu-guk"],["Mandu-gwa","dish/mandu-gwa"],["Meat jun","dish/meat-jun"],["Meju","dish/meju"],["Morkovcha","dish/morkovcha"],["Scorched rice","dish/scorched-rice"],["Seon (food)","dish/seon-food"],["Sungnyung","dish/sungnyung"],["Tteokbokki","dish/tteokbokki"],["Wakame","dish/wakame"],["Yakgwa","dish/yakgwa"],["Yukpo","dish/yukpo"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/52/Korean_Udon_%28Garakguksu%29.jpg/500px-Korean_Udon_%28Garakguksu%29.jpg","w":500,"h":350,"by":"Minseong Kim","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korean_Udon_(Garakguksu).jpg"}},"dish/hainanese-chicken-rice":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/hainanese-chicken-rice","n":"Hainanese chicken rice","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 8 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Hainanese chicken rice is a dish of poached or roasted chicken with seasoned rice, served with chilli sauce and usually with cucumbers, tomato slices or cilantro garnishes. It was created by immigrants from Hainan in southern China and adapted from the Hainanese dishes of Wenchang chicken and Wenchang chicken rice."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-hainanese-chicken-rice"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hainanese_chicken_rice"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-indonesian-cuisine"],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisine"],["Hainan cuisine","cuisine/hainan-cuisine"],["Indonesian Chinese cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-chinese-cuisine"],["Malaysian Chinese cuisine","cuisine/malaysian-chinese-cuisine"],["Singaporean cuisine","cuisine/singaporean-cuisine"],["Singaporean Chinese cuisine","cuisine/singaporean-cuisine-chinese-inspired"],["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Woks of Life","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thewoksoflife.com","note":"Chinese and Chinese American recipes from the Leung family, running since 2013.","url":"https://thewoksoflife.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Chinese Cooking Demystified","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"youtube.com","note":"Technique-heavy YouTube channel on regional Chinese home and restaurant cooking.","url":"https://www.youtube.com/@ChineseCookingDemystified","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"},{"name":"Red House Spice","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"redhousespice.com","note":"Classic Chinese recipes from Wei Guo, who grew up in Gansu, blogging since 2017.","url":"https://redhousespice.com/about/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Madame Huang's Kitchen: Henan cuisine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"carolynjphillips.blogspot.com","note":"Recipe posts on Hainan dishes from Carolyn Phillips' blog, Madame Huang's Kitchen.","url":"https://carolynjphillips.blogspot.com/search/label/Hainan%20cuisine","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/d4/Hainanese_chicken_rice.jpg/500px-Hainanese_chicken_rice.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Misaochan","lic":"CC BY 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hainanese_chicken_rice.jpg"}},"ingredient/bird-s-eye-chili":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/bird-s-eye-chili","n":"Bird's eye chili","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 11 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Bird's eye chili or Thai chili is a chili pepper variety from the species Capsicum annuum that is native to Mexico. Cultivated across Southeast Asia, it is used extensively in many Asian cuisines. It may be mistaken for a similar-looking chili derived from the species Capsicum frutescens, the cultivar siling labuyo. Capsicum frutescens fruits are generally smaller and characteristically point upwards. In the Marianas and Guam, these are often called boonie peppers or Doni Sali, which can be a term for regional wild (technically feral) varieties. The variation between different varieties can be significant for regional dishes or visuals, such as the Thai ornamental varieties."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-bird-s-eye-chili"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bird's_eye_chili"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Keumamah","dish/keumamah"],["Lawar (food)","dish/lawar-food"],["Lotek (food)","dish/lotek-food"],["Manuk napinadar","dish/manuk-napinadar"],["Mie caluk","dish/mie-caluk"],["Nasi tempong","dish/nasi-tempong"],["Natinombur","dish/natinombur"],["Otak-otak","dish/otak-otak"],["Rica-rica","dish/rica-rica"],["Sambal tuktuk","dish/sambal-tuktuk"],["Sate padang","dish/sate-padang"]]],["Acehnese cuisine","cuisine/acehnese-cuisine",[["Keumamah","dish/keumamah"],["Mie caluk","dish/mie-caluk"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Shallot","ingredient/shallot"],["Andaliman","ingredient/andaliman"],["Grated coconut","ingredient/grated-coconut"],["Lime","ingredient/lime"],["Kencur","ingredient/kencur"],["Tamarind","ingredient/tamarind"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/32/Thai_peppers.jpg/500px-Thai_peppers.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Daniel Risacher","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thai_peppers.jpg"}},"ingredient/pudding":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pudding","n":"Pudding","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Pudding is a food which can either be a dessert or a savoury dish. In the United States, pudding means a sweet, milk-based dessert similar in consistency to egg-based custards, instant custards or a mousse, often commercially set using cornstarch, gelatin or similar coagulating agent. These puddings are known in some Commonwealth countries as custards (or curds) if they are egg-thickened, as blancmange if starch-thickened, and as jelly if gelatin-based. Pudding in America may also refer to other dishes such as bread pudding and rice pudding, although typically these names derive from their origin as British dishes. The modern American meaning of pudding as dessert has evolved from the original almost exclusive use of the term to describe savoury dishes, specifically those created using a process similar to that used for sausages, in which meat and other ingredients in mostly liquid form"]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pudding"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pudding"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Kig ha farz","dish/kig-ha-farz"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Kig ha farz","dish/kig-ha-farz"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Buckwheat","ingredient/buckwheat"],["Buckwheat flour","ingredient/buckwheat-flour"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/26/Blanc-manger_on_glass_platter%2C_full_view.jpg/500px-Blanc-manger_on_glass_platter%2C_full_view.jpg","w":500,"h":640,"by":"SKopp","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blanc-manger_on_glass_platter%2C_full_view.jpg"}},"dish/nadugi":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/nadugi","n":"Nadugi","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Georgian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Nadugi is a traditional Georgian soft cheese appetizer or spread."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-nadugi"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nadugi"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Milk","ingredient/milk"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Georgian Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"georgianrecipes.net","note":"Recipe site dedicated to traditional Georgian dishes.","url":"https://georgianrecipes.net/about-georgian-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Georgian cuisine","links":[["Achma","dish/achma"],["Ajika","dish/ajika"],["Akhasheni","dish/akhasheni"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Chakapuli","dish/chakapuli"],["Chakhokhbili","dish/chakhokhbili"],["Chashushuli","dish/chashushuli"],["Chicken tabaka","dish/chicken-tabaka"],["Churchkhela","dish/churchkhela"],["Dambalkhacho","dish/dambalkhacho"],["Eggplant jam","dish/eggplant-jam"],["Gozinaki","dish/gozinaki"],["Khachapuri","dish/khachapuri"],["Kharcho","dish/kharcho"],["Khash (dish)","dish/khash-dish"],["Khvanchkara","dish/khvanchkara"],["Kindzmarauli","dish/kindzmarauli"],["Kubdari","dish/kubdari"],["Kuchmachi","dish/kuchmachi"],["Kvass","dish/kvass"],["Lobiani","dish/lobiani"],["Mchadi","dish/mchadi"],["Mujuretuli","dish/mujuretuli"],["Nigvziani badrijani","dish/nigvziani-badrijani"],["Pkhali","dish/pkhali"],["Shkmeruli","dish/shkmeruli"],["Tsolikouri","dish/tsolikouri"],["Tvishi (wine)","dish/tvishi-wine"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c0/Nadugi_with_mint_wrapped_in_sulguni.jpg/500px-Nadugi_with_mint_wrapped_in_sulguni.jpg","w":500,"h":365,"by":"Georgia About","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nadugi_with_mint_wrapped_in_sulguni.jpg"}},"dish/ramen-shop":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/ramen-shop","n":"Ramen shop","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese Chinese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["A ramen shop is a restaurant that specializes in ramen dishes, the wheat-flour Japanese noodles in broth. In Japan, ramen shops are very common and popular, and are sometimes referred to as ramen-ya or ramen-ten (ラーメン店). Some ramen shops operate in short-order style, while others provide patrons with sit-down service. Over 10,000 ramen shops exist in Japan, and the dish has gained popularity around the world."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ramen-shop"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramen_shop"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese Chinese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Sudachi Recipes: Wafu Pasta","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"sudachirecipes.com","note":"Chuka recipes such as gyoza, ebi mayo and tantanmen from Japanese cook Yuto Omura.","url":"https://sudachirecipes.com/chuka-ryori-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Just One Cookbook","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's collection of chuka dishes as cooked in Japanese homes.","url":"https://www.justonecookbook.com/japanese-chinese-food-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"No Recipes: Japanese Chinese","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"norecipes.com","note":"Japanese Chinese recipe category on Marc Matsumoto's food site.","url":"https://norecipes.com/recipe/cuisine/japanese-chinese/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Japanese Chinese cuisine","links":[["Chahan (dish)","dish/chahan-dish"],["Narutomaki","dish/narutomaki"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3f/%E6%9D%B1%E6%AD%A6%E3%82%89%E3%83%BC%E3%82%81%E3%82%9320240512-P1042538.jpg/500px-%E6%9D%B1%E6%AD%A6%E3%82%89%E3%83%BC%E3%82%81%E3%82%9320240512-P1042538.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"くろふね","lic":"CC BY 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%9D%B1%E6%AD%A6%E3%82%89%E3%83%BC%E3%82%81%E3%82%9320240512-P1042538.jpg"}},"dish/shirazi-salad":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/shirazi-salad","n":"Shirazi salad","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Iranian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Shirazi salad is a Persian salad that originated from and is named after Shiraz in southern Iran. It is a relatively modern dish, dating to sometime after the introduction of the tomato to Iran at the end of the nineteenth century in the Qajar era. Its primary ingredients are cucumber, tomato, onion, olive oil, herbal spices and verjuice, although lime juice is sometimes used in its preparation. In Iran, it is eaten in the summer as a side dish on its own, and year-round as a side dish alongside meat-based foods such as Persian kabob and as a side dish before and after meals. Shirazi salad is sometimes served as an accompaniment to rice such as loobia polo, an Iranian rice dish made with green beans and tomatoes. Cookbook author Jila Dana-Haeri describes it as a refreshing dish during the summer."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-shirazi-salad"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shirazi_salad"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Cucumber","ingredient/cucumber"],["Lime (fruit)","ingredient/lime-fruit"],["Mentha","ingredient/mentha"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Verjuice","ingredient/verjuice"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Unicorns in the Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"unicornsinthekitchen.com","note":"Iranian-born Shadi HasanzadeNemati's blog with a large Persian recipe section.","url":"https://www.unicornsinthekitchen.com/persian-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Iranian cuisine","links":[["Akuri","dish/akuri"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Chelow kabab","dish/chelow-kabab"],["Doogh","dish/doogh"],["Falooda","dish/falooda"],["Jujeh kabab","dish/jujeh-kabab"],["Kabab torsh","dish/kabab-torsh"],["Keşkek","dish/keskek"],["Khash (dish)","dish/khash-dish"],["Khoresh karafs","dish/khoresh-karafs"],["Lighvan cheese","dish/lighvan-cheese"],["Nisholda","dish/nisholda"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Scorched rice","dish/scorched-rice"],["Sheermal","dish/sheermal"],["Shirin polo","dish/shirin-polo"],["Sholezard","dish/sholezard"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/4/4b/%D8%B3%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AF_%D8%B4%DB%8C%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%B2%DB%8C.jpg","w":500,"h":711,"by":"Mohsenmosayebnejad","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%B3%D8%A7%D9%84%D8%A7%D8%AF_%D8%B4%DB%8C%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%B2%DB%8C.jpg"}},"dish/siomay":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/siomay","n":"Siomay","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Siomay (also somai) (Chinese: 燒賣; pinyin: shāo mài; Pe̍h-ōe-jī: sio-māi) is a Chinese-Indonesian steamed fish dumpling with vegetables served in peanut sauce. It is derived from the Chinese shumai. It is considered a light meal, similar to the Chinese dim sum. It is traditionally made from pork but is frequently substituted with tenggiri (Spanish mackerel), as many Indonesians observe the halal dietary law. Sometimes other types of seafood such as tuna, mackerel, and prawn or poultry such as chicken also can be used to make siomay."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-siomay"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siomay"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Peanut sauce","ingredient/peanut-sauce"],["Tapioca","ingredient/tapioca"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arem-arem","dish/arem-arem"],["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bekasang","dish/bekasang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Binte biluhuta","dish/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"],["Botok","dish/botok"],["Brem","dish/brem"],["Bubur kacang hijau","dish/bubur-kacang-hijau"],["Bubur ketan hitam","dish/bubur-ketan-hitam"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Burgo (food)","dish/burgo-food"],["Cendol","dish/cendol"],["Cenil","dish/cenil"],["Cilok","dish/cilok"],["Cimpa","dish/cimpa"],["Corn fritter","dish/corn-fritter"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dadar gulung","dish/dadar-gulung"],["Dadiah","dish/dadiah"],["Dengke mas naniura","dish/dengke-mas-naniura"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Emping","dish/emping"],["Es buah","dish/es-buah"],["Es doger","dish/es-doger"],["Es goyobod","dish/es-goyobod"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Geplak","dish/geplak"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/e/e2/Siomay_Bandung.jpg/500px-Siomay_Bandung.jpg","w":500,"h":367,"by":"Gunawan Kartapranata","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siomay_Bandung.jpg"}},"dish/suya":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/suya","n":"Suya","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Nigerian cuisine","dot":"#e46a3f","intro":[["Suya, also known as Tsire, is a traditional Hausa (Nigerian) smoke-grilled spiced meat on skewer. Suya is generally made with thin-sliced spiced beef, lamb, goat, ram, or chicken arranged on wooden skewers. Organ meats such as kidney, liver and tripe as well as other types of meats and seafood (shrimp) are also sometimes used. Suya is most popular as evening street food or snack, restaurant appetizer, and as accompaniment with drinks at bars and night spots."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-suya"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suya"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Nigerian cuisine","cuisine/nigerian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":7,"links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Cayenne pepper","ingredient/cayenne-pepper"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Goat","ingredient/goat"],["Ram","ingredient/ram"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Tripe","ingredient/tripe"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Kitchen Butterfly","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"kitchenbutterfly.com","note":"Long-running blog by Nigerian food writer and historian Ozoz Sokoh, with Nigerian and New Nigerian recipes.","url":"https://www.kitchenbutterfly.com/about-2","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Sisi Yemmie","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"sisiyemmie.com","note":"Nigerian home cooking recipes and meal plans, paired with the SisiYemmieTV YouTube channel.","url":"https://sisiyemmie.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Nigerian cuisine","links":[["Edikang ikong","dish/edikang-ikong"],["Edo black soup","dish/edo-black-soup"],["Miyar zogale","dish/miyar-zogale"],["Moin moin","dish/moin-moin"],["Ofada rice","dish/ofada-rice"],["Palm nut soup","dish/palm-nut-soup"],["Sinasir","dish/sinasir"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b4/Suya_with_pepper_sauce.jpg/500px-Suya_with_pepper_sauce.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Bukky658","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suya_with_pepper_sauce.jpg"}}}